-- Real user-reported false negative: `bounds.rs`'s write-obligation
-- checker (`Checker::expr_bound`) used to compose an `Expr::Call` to a
-- provable range ONLY when the callee declared an EXPLICIT `where`
-- return postcondition (v13, opt-in propagation only) -- a callee with
-- no such annotation composed to `None` unconditionally, "cannot verify
-- this write stays within the declared bound," even when the call was
-- provably safe.
--
-- `Double`'s return type (`[n + 1]`) is a generic WIDTH guarantee, not
-- a value-range refinement -- there was no way to attach a meaningful
-- postcondition to it at all (a return bound needs a concretely-known
-- `bits[N]` width, which a generic type doesn't have until a call
-- site). `b`'s own inductive bound (`_ > 1`, narrowed to `[2, 15)` by
-- the guard `b < 0b1111`) DOES prove `Double(trunc(b, 4))` stays in
-- range (`[4, 29)`, well within `[2, 32)`) -- but only once three
-- capabilities compose: `trunc`'s own identity-when-it-already-fits
-- shape, `Double`'s `x << 1` body composing via `Shl`, and body-
-- substitution inlining (binding `Double`'s own param `x` to the call's
-- actual argument range and recursing into its single-`return` body
-- directly, exactly as if it were expanded in place at the call site --
-- which is literally what FIRRTL emission already does for a generic
-- call).
module M {
    in a : [1]
    out b : [5] where _ > 1 = 5

    Double(x : [n]) : [n + 1] { return x << 1 }

    rule step {
        a?
        b < 0b1111
        b := Double(trunc(b, 4))
    }
}
